Build succeeded. Now I need to check if the SchemaForm handles the schema correctly. The backend returns something like:

```json
{
  "zeroclaw": {
    "chat_form_schema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "provider": {
          "type": "string",
          "enum": ["factory", "ollama", "openrouter", ...],
          "description": "LLM provider route"
        },
        "model": {
          "type": "string",
          "oneOf": [
            {"const": "auto", "title": "auto (Factory auto-router)"},
            {"const": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6", "title": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.6 (code)"},
            ...
          ],
          "description": "Model to use for this turn"
        },
        "message": {
          "type": "string",
          "description": "Chat message",
          "minLength": 1
        }
      },
      "required": ["message"]
    }
  }
}
```

The SchemaForm component handles:
- `enum` -> select dropdown (for provider)
- `oneOf` with const/title -> select dropdown (for model)
- `type: "string"` with no enum -> textarea (for message)

This should work! But there's one issue - when the provider changes, the model options need to be filtered. Currently the backend returns ALL models in `oneOf`, not filtered by provider.

Looking at the handler code, it builds model_routes like:
```rust
let model_routes = zeroclaw
    .get("model_routes")
    .and_then(|v| v.as_array())
    .map(|arr| {
        arr.iter()
            .filter_map(|r| {
                let model = r.get("model").and_then(|v| v.as_str())?;
                let hint = r.get("hint").and_then(|v| v.as_str());
                let label = hint.map(|h| format!("{} ({h})", model)).unwrap_or_else(|| model.to_string());
                Some(json!({"const": model, "title": label}))
            })
            .collect::<Vec<_>>()
    })
    .unwrap_or_default();
```

So the schema has ALL model routes in the `oneOf` array, but the ChatPage tries to filter them by provider when provider changes. 

The filtering logic in ChatPage updates the schema dynamically when the provider selection changes. It filters the `oneOf` array in the schema based on the selected provider, narrowing down the available model options to those relevant to the chosen provider. This ensures that when a user switches providers, the model dropdown updates to show only compatible models.

